 {:seon.task/name "a-message-is-delivered-when-it-is-on-the-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09 (docs/decisions.md, a message is delivered when it is on the recipient's branch): \"just deliver the message and the message being in the agents branch is the proof of delivery\". Agreed by Web, Context and Optimization; Context builds it; it lands in the store-erase window of Optimization's config entity (stage 3's B), merged into B's branch or landed right after it, and before jolt/sync (stage 5), so a syncing pull never meets :seon.message/call.

What's wrong (Optimization's reproduction, on two branches of the world with :all: kb owed [\"m-two\"] before its pull and nothing after): :seon.message/call holds each recipient's turn on the one message entity all recipients share, so a sync brings another recipient's call down and the message reads as taken.

The change: seon.task/undelivered answers the messages main holds to k whose id k's branch does not hold (an id lookup in place of the call test); a message the agent removed (its ref retracted, its entity kept) stays delivered, one on main when the branch forked is delivered at the fork. seon.agent/own-message-tx and entries-tx write no call, deliver! takes no turn (its callers in seon.turn move with it). :seon.message/call goes: its schema entry, the agent pattern's {:seon.message/_call [:db/id]}, seon.ctx.session's session-kinds message entry. The turn that took a message is derived: the first turn whose basis t is at or after its seon.ctx/first-delivered (the tx of its :seon.message/to datom on the branch); seon.ctx/turn-members and seon.ctx.wire/turn-entities (stage 2b) read it so. A message delivered mid-turn now reads at the next turn's start, the order it arrived in (today it reads inside the turn in force, before the reply it came after): named in the landing. Docs: users.md, turn.md, data-modeling.md. Estimate (m the messages to the agent, c its turns): undelivered O(m) seeks on main and O(m) id lookups on the branch; a turn's messages O(m) first-delivered seeks and O(m log c) placing them against the bases. Being built 2026-11-09 by a helper on jolt/remodel-4 (.worktrees/remodel-4), reviewed by Context before landing.

Proofs (restated by Web under the owner's rule): synced, the message brought onto kb's branch by a merge is delivered and kb's next turn after that merge reads it once; private (nothing from main after the fork), kb is owed it until deliver! writes it; ka's taking it changes nothing for kb; a message delivered mid-turn reads at the next turn's start; test/seon/recorded_requests.edn byte for byte.

Status 2026-11-09: built and reviewed on jolt/remodel-4 at e4c89f7915 (.worktrees/remodel-4; helper's 43fec2ee4e, then Context's review fix), audited by Web (worth it). seon.ctx/placed-messages places every turn's messages in one range read of :seon.message/to and one pass over the turns' edges (the :t of a turn's basis, else the transaction that made its call; content-hash eids carry no order, so nothing orders by them); seon.ctx/turn-evals and turn-members (one turn's) stay for one-turn readers; seon.render.ui/conversation-list places once and hands each turn its messages; a message delivered after the last turn's edge shows in a :pending slot until the next turn opens. Proofs, red on main first: seon.agent-test/a-message-synced-from-main-is-delivered-and-read-by-the-next-turn, seon.task-test/a-message-on-the-branch-is-delivered, seon.ctx.wire-test/a-mid-turn-message-reads-at-the-next-turn-and-the-prefix-holds; and the taken-by-one-agent and private-branch cases. 342 tests green across what it reaches; test/seon/recorded_requests.edn byte for byte. Measured placed-messages: 4.5k ticks and 13 KB at 10 turns, 10k at 40, 16k at 160, 34k ticks and 133 KB at 640 (3 contracted calls; the helper's 0.63 MB at 640 turns and 213 messages); the step cost proof seon.render-cost-test/a-step-costs-what-changed holds within its bound (a simpler merge sorting the calls each step went 1.29x over at 640 and was not kept). src +106 -60. Next: Optimization makes jolt/erase-window from main when its cite-ref is ready; Context merges jolt/remodel-4 into it; Optimization proves the combination once and lands it with one store erase (the old :seon.message/call datoms go with it). The landing names: the mid-turn message reading at the next turn, the measured numbers, src delta.

Status, later 2026-11-09: main aa08b56b21 (index-arg, Jolt pin 3754a15e) merged into jolt/remodel-4 (5020ec09e3, then a commit restoring main's pin, which the merge had recorded as the worktree's old checkout). With the pin synced and the binary rebuilt, its tests stop at the seed: seon.source/tx-of answers :seon.source/unanalyzable \"var: #'seon.db/index is not public\" importing 28 changed files (seon.db/index is public and the same as main's); reported to Optimization as an import-order question in index-arg; Optimization fixed it on main (a dependent read again takes the privacy of every file the refresh read). Main merged again (2848eac52b) and the proofs' helper given the index (0f0211921c): every reached test green (seon.ctx-test, seon.agent-test, seon.task-test, seon.ctx.wire-test, seon.render-test, seon.render-cost-test). Next: merged into jolt/erase-window once Optimization makes it."
